# Reconciliator design

How a program of the Fearless family comes to hold a set of extensions, and makes the desktop of the system show the icons of those extensions and of the windows of that program.
The manager (see ControllersDesign) and the portable Fearless call the Reconciliator. The Reconciliator knows nothing of projects and mains: the caller gives the extensions, the icons and the windows.

The algorithms of this design are a contract on the behaviour: a specification, not a suggested implementation.
They may be very slow or otherwise inefficient. They are designed to be obviously correct; implementing them as they are may be a terrible idea.

### The data

The 'identity' of a program is the file name of the launcher of that program, without extension.
The 'Fearless family' is the set of the identities containing the text `earless`.
Each version of the manager and each version of the portable Fearless has an identity of its own.
The program folder of a manager has two launchers, `<name>` and `<name>w`: `<name>` and `<name>w` are two members of the Fearless family, with the identities `<name>` and `<name>w`, and one manager folder.
The kind of an extension follows from the text of the extension: `.fearless`, `.fapp` and three digits, and `.ffile` and three digits are Fearless extensions; every other extension is a system extension.
A program 'holds' an extension when the desktop of the system opens the files with that extension with that program, on a double click. Refusals gives the program holding an extension on each system.
```
ExtensionIcon: { .extension: FileExtension; .icon: FsPath }    // the .ico on Windows, the PNG on Linux and macOS
WindowIcon: { .windowClass: Str; .name: Str; .target: FsPath; .png: FsPath }  // Linux only
Reconciliator: {
  mut .reconcile(sys: mut System, identity: Str, name: Str, command: FsPath, extensions: List[ExtensionIcon], taken: List[FileExtension], windows: List[WindowIcon], programIcon: FsPath, replaced: mut MF[FsPath, Void]): Void;
  mut .eradicateAll(sys: mut System): Void;      // deletes the setup of every identity of the Fearless family, with no check
  }
```
`.reconcile` makes `command` the program holding each extension of `extensions`, under `identity`, each extension with its icon, and installs the icons of `windows`.
`.reconcile` gives no description of its own to the type of an extension: the desktop shows its own text for each extension. The mains of a manager may claim the same extension, so no one description fits an extension.
`programIcon` is the icon of `command` itself: the PNG on Linux and macOS. Windows ignores `programIcon` and uses the icon inside `command`.
`name` is the name the desktop shows for `command`: `Fearless Manager` for a manager, `Fearless Portable` for the portable Fearless. `name` is the `Name` of the `.desktop` file on Linux, the `ApplicationName` on Windows and the `CFBundleName` of the 'helper bundle' on macOS.
The 'setup' of an identity is every registry key and file `.reconcile` writes for that identity.
After `.reconcile`, the setup of `identity` names exactly the extensions of `extensions`.
An identity 'releases' an extension when the setup of the identity stops naming the extension.
The setup of every other identity of the Fearless family, and the setup of `identity` when that setup has another command, is deleted, after `replaced` is called on the command of that setup.
A `.reconcile` with the arguments of the previous `.reconcile` of `identity` changes no setup.
Every `.reconcile` that passes its checks ends with one refresh of the desktop (`SHChangeNotify` on Windows, the MIME and desktop databases on Linux, `LSRegisterURL` of the 'helper bundle' on macOS), also when no setup changed: a changed image at the same path then shows too.
The checks of `.reconcile` change nothing when the checks fail.

### Refusals

The checks of `.reconcile` refuse in two cases:
- more than one identity of the Fearless family is on the system: the error lists those identities;
- a program outside the Fearless family holds an extension of `extensions` that is not in `taken`: the error maps each such extension to the program holding that extension.
Each extension of `taken` is an extension of `extensions`.
`.reconcile` makes `command` hold each extension of `taken`, and each extension of `extensions` that another program of the Fearless family holds:
- On Windows the program holding an extension is the ProgId of the UserChoice of the extension (the keys `UserChoice` and `UserChoiceLatest`), or, with no UserChoice, the ProgId of the default value of `HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\<ext>`. `.reconcile` deletes the UserChoice of each of those extensions. A UserChoice that `.reconcile` deletes does not come back when the identity releases the extension. `.reconcile` keeps the previous default value of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes\<ext>`, and puts that value back when the identity releases the extension.
- On Linux the program holding an extension is the default program of the desktop for the type of the glob of highest weight matching the name `x<ext>` in the MIME database; no program holds an extension that no glob matches. `.reconcile` removes, from every `mimeapps.list` and `<desktop>-mimeapps.list` file the desktop reads (`<desktop>` is each name of `$XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP`, in lower case), each line, in any section, whose key is the type of the identity for one of those extensions (see Extensions on Linux).
- On macOS the program holding an extension is the application macOS opens a file named `x<ext>` with. `.reconcile` asks macOS to make the 'helper bundle' of the identity (for `.fearless`, the application of `command`) the default program of the type of each of those extensions (see Extensions on macOS); macOS asks the user, and `.reconcile` waits for the answer. An extension that the user keeps for another program makes `.reconcile` refuse after writing the setup of the identity: the error maps each such extension to the program holding that extension.

### The setup on each system

The setup is the same for every version of every program of the Fearless family: each program of the Fearless family finds, reads and deletes the setup of the other identities.
On Windows the setup of an identity is:
- the key `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\<identity>`, with `Capabilities` (the values `ApplicationName` and `ApplicationIcon`, and the key `FileAssociations` mapping each extension to the ProgId of that extension) and `PreviousDefaults` (the previous default value of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes\<ext>` for each extension, by extension);
- the value `<identity>` of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\RegisteredApplications`, naming `Software\<identity>\Capabilities`;
- for each extension, the ProgId `<identity>.<ext without dot>` in `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes`, with the icon of the extension as `DefaultIcon` and `"<command>" "%1"` as `shell\open\command`, named by the default value of `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Classes\<ext>` and by the `OpenWithProgids` of that key.
On Windows the identities on the system are the value names of `RegisteredApplications` and the names `<name>` of the keys `HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\<name>\Capabilities`, that are in the Fearless family.
On Windows the command of a setup is the command in `shell\open\command` of the ProgIds of that setup.
On Windows the name of the ProgId `<name>.<ext without dot>` of an extension is `<name>`; the name of every other ProgId is the whole ProgId.
On Linux the setup of an identity is `<identity>.desktop` and the `.desktop` file of each window class in `$XDG_DATA_HOME/applications`, the MIME package of the identity (see Extensions on Linux), and the icons of the identity (see Icons on the desktop).
On Linux the identities on the system are the file names, without extension and without a final `-` followed by hex digits, of the `.desktop` files in the `applications` folder of `$XDG_DATA_HOME` and of each folder of `$XDG_DATA_DIRS`, and of the files in the `mime/packages` folder of those same folders, that are in the Fearless family.
On Linux the command of a setup is the `TryExec` of `<identity>.desktop`.
On Linux the name of a program is the file name of the `.desktop` file of that program without `.desktop`.
On macOS the command of a setup is the command the launcher script of the 'helper bundle' runs (see Extensions on macOS).
On macOS the name of an application is the file name of that application without `.app`.
A program holding an extension is in the Fearless family when the name of that program contains the text `earless`.

### Extensions on Linux

On Linux a program opens MIME types, not extensions; the shared MIME database turns a file name into a type through name patterns (globs). Several extensions can share one type (`*.htm` and `*.html` are both `text/html`), so taking over a type would take over all the extensions of that type. `.reconcile` never takes over a type: `.reconcile` gives each extension a type of its own, in the MIME package of the identity (the file `$XDG_DATA_HOME/mime/packages/<identity>.xml`, which only `.reconcile` writes):
```
<mime-type type="application/x-fearless-htm">
  <glob pattern="*.htm" weight="100"/>
  <sub-class-of type="text/html"/>
  <sub-class-of type="application/xhtml+xml"/>
  <icon name="<identity>-<hash>"/>
</mime-type>
```
- The glob weight is 100, above every glob shipped by the systems we target (at most 80), so the name `x.htm` turns into the type of the identity and `x.html` stays `text/html`.
- When a system glob also matches the name, GIO and Qt look at the file content to choose, and text content would go to the system type. The `sub-class-of` entries, one for each type the system database gives to `*.htm`, make the content of those types count as the type of the identity. A file whose content is unmistakably another format (a `.htm` file starting with `<?php`) still gets that other type; this is accepted.
- The `.desktop` of the identity lists the types of the identity; no other program lists those types, so a double click on a `.htm` file reaches `command`, and every other program opening `text/html` stays in the "open with" choices.
- `.fearless` keeps its type `application/x-fearless`. Every other extension gets `application/x-fearless-<ext without dot>`.
- Releasing an extension rewrites the MIME package of the identity without that extension, and the system types are back as they were.

### Extensions on macOS

On macOS a program opens types, not extensions: an application declares, in the `Info.plist` of the application, the types the application opens, and the extensions of the types the application invents.
Changing the `Info.plist` of a signed application breaks the signature of that application.
So the application of `command` declares `.fearless` when that application is built, and `.reconcile` declares every other extension in the 'helper bundle' of the identity.
The 'helper bundle' of an identity is the application `~/Library/Application Support/fearlessAssociations/<identity>.app`, which only `.reconcile` writes: an `Info.plist`, a launcher script running `command` with the one argument `FearlessCommandOpened`, the icon of each extension and the icon of `command`.
The 'helper bundle' is the setup of the identity: the identities on the system are the 'helper bundles' in `fearlessAssociations`, and `.reconcile` writes the 'helper bundle' also when `.fearless` is the only extension.
The 'declared type' of an extension is the first type macOS gives for that extension that a bundle outside `fearlessAssociations` declares.
- An extension with no 'declared type' gets a type of its own: `<identity><ext>`, with `-` for each `_` and in lower case, because a type name has no `_` and macOS reads type names in lower case. The 'helper bundle' declares that type and opens that type as owner (`LSHandlerRank` `Owner`), so a double click on a file of that extension reaches `command`.
- An extension with a 'declared type' gets no new type: the 'helper bundle' opens the 'declared type' as alternate (`LSHandlerRank` `Alternate`). `command` is then in the "Open With" choices of every extension of the 'declared type' (of `.html` too when the extension is `.htm`). macOS keeps one default program for each type, so holding such an extension makes `command` hold every extension of the 'declared type'.
- Deleting the setup of an identity registers the 'helper bundle' with an `Info.plist` declaring no type, then deletes the 'helper bundle'.
- After the identity releases an extension, macOS chooses the program holding that extension.

### Icons on the desktop

On Windows the registry names an icon by its path, so the desktop reads the `.icon` of each `ExtensionIcon` from that path. On Linux a MIME type names its icon, and the desktop looks names up only in the folders of the icon theme specification: `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons` of the user, `$XDG_DATA_DIRS/icons` and `/usr/share/pixmaps` of the system (root only) and the legacy `~/.icons`. `.reconcile` installs each icon as `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons/hicolor/256x256/mimetypes/<identity>-<hash>.png`; the hash gives a changed image a new name, past the icon cache of the desktop. Every file there named `<identity>-<hash>.png` belongs to that identity: each `.reconcile` deletes the files of that identity that the `.reconcile` does not install. `.reconcile` installs `programIcon` as `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons/hicolor/256x256/apps/<identity>.png`.
On Linux each window class of `windows` gets a `.desktop` file `<identity>-<hash>.desktop`, with `<hash>` the lower case hex digits of a hash of the window class, with `NoDisplay=true`, with `.name` as its name, `command` followed by `.target` as its command, the path of the installed icon as its icon and the window class as its `StartupWMClass`: GNOME and COSMIC show that icon for every window of that window class.
On macOS the `Info.plist` of the 'helper bundle' names an icon file for each type: `.reconcile` writes the icon of each extension as `Contents/Resources/<ext without dot>.icns`, an icns file containing the PNG scaled to 256 pixels. Finder shows the icon of a new extension at once. After the icon of an extension changes, Finder keeps showing the first icon of that extension until the icon store of macOS is rebuilt.

### Notes

- The code of the Reconciliator is today the package `fileAssociations` of Commons (`FileAssociations`, `WindowsAssociations`, `LinuxAssociations`, `MacAssociations`, `Ico`, `Icon`, `WindowIcon`, `Shell`, `Xdg`), called by `Controllers/src/controller/Association.java` and by `offerAssociation` of `Coordinator/src/mainCoordinator/Main.java`. That code becomes a component of its own, called from the repositories Controllers and Coordinator.
- The exact checks and steps of `.reconcile` are those of the doc comment of `FileAssociations` in Commons.
- `Ico` makes the `.ico` of an icon from the PNG of the icon; `._writeIcons` of CoordinatorDesign and the manager (Conflict icon in ControllersDesign) need `Ico`.
- The code passes both an `.ico` and a PNG for each extension (`Icon`), where the design passes only the file the system reads.
- A `.reconcile` that finds no setup of the identity and is given no extension changes nothing and refreshes nothing: on Linux the tools refreshing the MIME and desktop databases fail on folders that do not exist.
